# Ported 2026-10-13 (branch large-message-headers): 5841e577e, upstream issue
# #683, merged as PR #686 - a message over 80 MB. Here the write of such a
# message was already refused (MessageData::CheckWritable_, HM6010), so the blank
# message #683 reports never went out; what was left was the defect's other half,
# fixed in our code for the paths it names, with tests that fail on 74aed9ab8:
# the rule-loop count that an account's forward, a Sieve redirect, a rule's
# forward and copy and a bounce read and raise, the rules' header conditions and
# header actions, the spam headers of a message marked as spam and their removal
# from an account's copy, and the signature and the disclaimers, which are not
# appended to such a message and say so in the log
# (hmailserver/docs/MailFlowRules.md, "A message over 80 MB"). Upstream's
# SignatureAdder refusal is taken, with the log line beside it.
#
#   DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN:
#   - The header-only load for EVERY caller once the file is over 80 MB
#     (MessageData::LoadFromMessage). Ours reads the header alone only for a
#     caller that asks (MessageData::LargeMessage::HeaderOnly): the other callers -
#     the virus scanner, the sandbox, the indexer, IMAP SEARCH, the REST routes,
#     COM's Message and more - would otherwise see a message with no body and no
#     attachments where they now see a load refused, and a scan of its
#     attachments would find nothing in a message it never read.
#   - Its Events.cs case, a script's header change saved over COM on such a
#     message (OnAcceptMessage, Message.HeaderValue, Message.Save). COM's Message
#     still refuses a message over 80 MB, so the change is not saved (HM6010) and
#     the message is kept as it was. Reading it header only there would change what
#     every script sees of such a message - its header, where it now sees none -
#     which is the owner's to decide. Until then a rule's Run function is not run
#     on such a message - its Message would show the script an empty one - and
#     the log says so (RuleApplier::ApplyAction_).
#   - Upstream's error numbers 5736-5738 (ours are HM7840-HM7842) and its own
#     temporary-file copy of the body in MimeBody::SaveAllToFile: ours copies it
#     in pieces through FileUtilities::WriteToFileAtomically, the writer every
#     rewrite of a message here goes through.
#
# Ported 2026-10-12 (branch dns-failed-lookup): 8141d66af, upstream PR #640,
# adapted - a DNS lookup that failed was read as "no records". An address lookup
# asks A and AAAA and was true when either query was answered, so a SERVFAIL or
# a timeout on A beside AAAA answered NODATA - every IPv4-only host's answer -
# was a name with no addresses: the HELO test scored what it could not look up,
# and a DNS blacklist or SURBL read the failure as "not listed". Upstream's rule
# is written into DNSResolver::GetIpAddressesRecursive_, with a status for each
# query: false unless addresses were found or every A and AAAA query was
# answered. The DNS blacklists and SURBL ask through
# DNSResolver::GetDnsListAnswers - the A record alone (RFC 5782), asked once
# more past the DNS cache when it fails, which is upstream's second attempt -
# and DNSResolverWinApi::Query takes upstream's bypassCache on our DnsQueryEx
# path (the POSIX resolver keeps no cache). SURBL passes over a name it could
# not look up in our concurrent SURBL::Run, as upstream's loop skips it; BLCheck
# writes upstream's "DNS lookup failed" line in place of "0 addresses found",
# where upstream writes both, and reports to AntiSpamDiagnostics. Beyond
# upstream: a CNAME query that fails after an answer whose records were not kept
# (on Windows the answer for an alias, which DNSResolverWinApi::RunQuery_ does
# not keep under the name asked) fails the address lookup,
# GetRecordsOfTypeRecursive_ and GetMXRecordsRecursive_, where upstream counts
# the CNAME query in none of them and leaves the record lookups as they were;
# after NODATA it changes nothing, as before, and a name answered NXDOMAIN is
# asked none. SpamTestPTR scores no reverse name whose forward lookup failed, as
# it scores no PTR lookup that failed (upstream leaves SpamTestPTR as it was;
# the owner kept it on 13 October 2026). GetEmailServersRecursive_ takes an
# address two exchanges share out on its failure return too, which the rule
# sends more exchanges to. AntiSpam/DnsLookupFailures.cs holds the spam tests,
# the delivery notice and the MX query to it, its cases of the defect failing on
# 74aed9ab8, and DNSResolverTester the lookups, where each part of the fix has a
# case that fails with it taken out.
#
#   DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN:
#   - 8141d66af (#640), DNSResolver::GetIpAddressesWithRetry, which asks A and
#     AAAA twice: GetDnsListAnswers serves the only callers that retry, BLCheck
#     and SURBL, and asks the A record alone. Under the new rule an AAAA query
#     that failed beside a list's answer would fail a lookup the list answered,
#     and a list that does not answer would be waited for four timeouts where
#     two were waited for before.
#   - 8141d66af (#640), GetIpAddresses' bypassCache parameter: no other caller
#     asks an address lookup past the cache, and GetDnsListAnswers passes
#     bypassCache to DNSResolverWinApi::Query itself.
#
#   NOT APPLICABLE:
#   - 8141d66af (#640), VMTestRunner.Console/TestEnvironments.json: it enables
#     the machines of upstream's own test lab. Ours lists those of the 6.0
#     baseline, and is neither its pre-image nor its post-image.
